  <h2>What is KernelSU?</h2>
  <p>
    KernelSU is an open-source rooting solution created by tiann. Where Magisk
    patches the boot image from userspace, KernelSU works directly in kernel
    space on devices that ship with a GKI (Generic Kernel Image) kernel: the
    kernel itself is what grants superuser access to apps, managed through the
    KernelSU manager app.
  </p>

  <h2>What it gives you</h2>
  <p>
    Superuser (root) access with per-app profiles — including per-app
    capabilities and mount-namespace rules — plus a module system compatible
    with many <a href="/magisk">Magisk</a> modules, so much of the existing
    module ecosystem carries over.
  </p>

  <h2>How it differs from Magisk</h2>
  <p>
    Both are systemless in spirit: the operating-system partition stays
    untouched. The difference is where the root logic lives — Magisk injects it
    during boot from a patched boot image, while KernelSU's lives in the kernel
    itself, which is why KernelSU only targets GKI-kernel devices.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you root</h2>
  <p>
    KernelSU requires an unlocked bootloader, and rooting trips integrity
    checks that some apps — banking and payment apps above all — actively look
    for. Download KernelSU <em>only</em> from the official GitHub page; files
    from unknown sources can be malicious. Knowing your exact device and
    bootloader state is step one — our free
    <a href="/scan">browser-based scan</a> reports both over USB.
  </p>
